    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's really helpful for the methodology section. I'm also looking at how these chemical variables translate to actual cooking practicalities. For instance, why does adding a little bit of baking soda sometimes make meat brown so much faster, but if you use too much, it can end up with a weird, bitter taste or an off-texture? Is there a way to explain that specifically through the lens of the reaction kinetics you mentioned?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The addition of baking soda, which is sodium bicarbonate, is essentially a rapid and significant shift towards an alkaline environment, which directly accelerates the Maillard reaction kinetics. This faster reaction rate is why you see quicker browning, as the alkaline conditions dramatically speed up the Amadori rearrangement and subsequent condensation steps.\n\nHowever, the bitterness and off-texture when too much is used are a direct consequence of pushing the reaction too far, too fast. When the alkalinity is excessive, the degradation pathways become overly aggressive, leading to the formation of undesirable, highly reactive, and sometimes bitter-tasting side products. Furthermore, extreme pH shifts can sometimes cause undesirable protein denaturation or structural changes in the meat itself, affecting the texture.",
      "source": "target",
